{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310833,"nonce":"S1Q8fUM8cR2q4f9iwefur0p88qo=","title":"FIXED 2026-09-05 in dee41319 by the INLINER (bind an erased slot to the argument BITS + record every erased slot), not by call-site type ...","message":"Filling `type_arguments` in the TYPER (a structural bind of declared params\nagainst actual argument types, in `expr/calls.rs`, at both the receiver path and\nthe bare-name path) had NO effect: the monomorphizer still logged\n`NO explicit type_args`. The probe explains why and is the lead worth keeping:\n\n    [typearg] eq params=[Abstract{Int64}, Abstract{Int64}] argc=2\n\n**The bare call `eq(...)` inside a `unit.Test` subclass resolves, IN THE TYPER,\nto `haxe.Int64.eq` — a two-parameter namesake — not to `unit.Test.eq`.** MIR\nresolves it correctly (it calls the 3-param `[Ptr(Void), I64, I64]` template),\nso execution is right and only the TYPE information is wrong. Any type-argument\ninference keyed on the typer's `method_symbol` is therefore working from the\nwrong method, and no amount of unification will help until that collision is\nfixed. Also note: a type parameter is modelled as `TypeKind::Abstract` over a\n`SymbolKind::Abstract` symbol with `GENERIC=false`, so there is no flag that\ndistinguishes `T` from a real abstract — resolve the collision first.\n\n34 of the 82 remaining `uncompiled` rows are this cluster (16 aeq, 15 eq, 3 both).\n\n**Battery position at ffda14de:** 439/439 vs the 439 baseline — GAINED Issue3063 (CRASH-\u003ePASS),\nLOST Issue12700 (PASS-\u003eWRONG_ANSWER `1 != 1`). The row's old PASS was luck: before the anon\n`.length` fix it read uninitialised memory and scored `1 != 0.000…` on most runs. The eq path was\nalways broken; it now fails honestly and deterministically.\n\n**Evidence:** `$SP/hf12700` (Test.hx `eq\u003cT\u003e` → shim Assert.equals(Dynamic, Dynamic); Main extends Test).\nAfter 2026-09-05's by-tag boxing (`haxe_box_value_by_tag`, tag fixup placeholder in\n`maybe_box_value_inner`) and the imported-callee `Dynamic` harvest, the replica no longer SIGSEGVs\nbut prints `equals: 1 != 0.000…` — both operands boxed with tag 0 (template unspecialized), so the\nf64 bits print as denormals. MIR: `fn @Test.eq($1: i64, $2: i64)` erased; `call fn330002($0, bits, bits)`\ncarries NO type args; `direct.rs` `call_type_args` = HIR call type args (always empty) OR receiver\nclass type args (Test has none) ⇒ no `MonoKey`, no specialization.\n\nThree coupled edits: (a) runtime `haxe_box_value_by_tag(bits, tag)` + a TypeParameter arm in\n`maybe_box_value_inner` using a `type_param_tag_fixups` placeholder; (b) `compilation.rs` harvest\nrecording `Dynamic` formals and unconstrained params as `\u003cT\u003e`; (c) `direct.rs`\n`infer_type_args_from_args`. RESULT: hf12700 still wrong (tag stays 0 — the monomorphizer never\nspecialised `Test.eq`, so (c) did not bind: `function_param_hir_types` has the INHERITED method\nunder a different id, and the recorded names were absent), and Issue11317 + Issue12213 REGRESSED\nto WRONG_ANSWER. Cause of the regressions: BOTH new branches `return` early from\n`maybe_materialize_for_call`, skipping the argument handling that followed (anon materialization,\ninterface wraps). Issue12213 stayed broken even after narrowing the boxing and falling through,\nso the HARVEST ALONE changes behaviour: adding entries to `import_function_param_iface_names` for\nfunctions that had none flips `contains_key` guards elsewhere.\nRULES LEARNED: (1) never `return` from `maybe_materialize_for_call` on a new branch unless it did\nsomething; fall through. (2) Adding rows to a harvest map is not neutral — guards test presence.\n(3) A/B each edit of a coupled unit separately: three reverts were needed to find which one hurt.\n\n**Plan (MIR-side, uses existing plumbing):**\n1. `compilation.rs` harvest (`resolve_name`, ~7130): record an unconstrained `TypeParameter` param\n   as `Some(\"\u003cT\u003e\")` (its NAME) — today `_ =\u003e None`. Local callees already have\n   `function_param_hir_types` with the TypeParameter kinds.\n2. `direct.rs` where `call_type_args` is computed: if `has_type_params` and both sources are empty,\n   bind each name in `module.functions[func_id].signature.type_params` (DECLARATION ORDER — the\n   monomorphizer zips this list with type_args) from the first param whose recorded name is `\u003cT\u003e`\n   (or whose HIR type is `TypeParameter{T}`) using `convert_type(args[i].ty)`; unbound ⇒ no type_args.\n3. The specialized signature stays erased (`substitute_signature` only rewrites `TypeVar`, and `T`\n   params are already I64), so call sites keep passing raw bits — ABI unchanged; only the tag\n   constants change ⇒ `haxe_box_value_by_tag(bits, 4)` boxes 1.0 as Float.\n4. Verify with `$SP/hf12700` (expect ok|ok|ok), the corpus row Issue12700 (`--single-target`),\n   then the battery. Typer-side alternative (fill `type_arguments` in calls.rs via\n   `match_type_param_in_types`) is cleaner but the declaration ORDER of a method's type params is\n   not stored on the symbol (no `function_type_params` table) — would need one.\nTRAP: the Cranelift tier stubs any function whose signature/locals carry `IrType::TypeVar`\n(`references_type_params`); specializations must leave none.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_generic_method_type_args_never_inferred` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}